// @contract: C-105
// #2310's gate: the accumulator `acc + [x]` over a HEAP element grows in place,
// in both spellings. Before the fix the in-place append window refused a 4-byte
// handle slot and the concat path copied the whole list per step — 100,000
// String appends took 10.5 s against 11 ms for `Int`, and a 200,000-element
// accumulator reached C-197 (#2291 had to write `list.iterate` with `list.push`
// because of it).
//
// TWO THINGS ARE PINNED HERE, and both were checked by breaking them.
//
// 1. The growth is in place. Measured A/B against develop without #2310 (same
//    fixture, same machine): allocation watermark 721,440 B before against
//    459,328 B after, wall time 0.78 s before against under 10 ms after —
//    while stdout is IDENTICAL on both, so the prints below cannot see it. The
//    catching gate is the allocation ledger, which is an EQUALITY pin, so the
//    1.6x drift fails as loudly as 1000x would
//    (crates/almide-wasm/tests/golden/alloc-baseline.txt). Note the memory
//    number's shape: a per-step spine copy at n = 20,000 COPIES 4 · n²/2 ≈
//    800 MB but does not ALLOCATE it — outgrown spines are recycled through the
//    free-list size classes (exact allocations only start above the 512 KB
//    class, #1701) — so the watermark moves 1.6x and max RSS barely moves
//    (9.2 MB against 9.0 MB). An earlier draft of this comment claimed ~800 MB
//    of allocation; measuring it is what corrected that.
//
// 2. The observable equivalence of both spellings at volume, over a heap
//    element: every element is a COMPUTED string (not a static-pool literal),
//    every element is READ BACK through `total_len`, and both ends of both
//    lists are printed, so a spine that loses or duplicates an element shows up
//    as a wrong length, a wrong total, or wrong bytes on one leg only.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT CATCH, established by mutation rather than
// assumed: deleting `rc_share_guard` from
// `stmts_append.rs::try_list_append_assign` and rebuilding leaves this
// fixture's stdout and watermark IDENTICAL on both legs. That mutant is killed
// by `list_index_element_append.almd`'s allocation row instead. Two reasons,
// both structural: the loop elements are born here, so they are OWNED and move
// into the spine with no +1 to lose — the guard only matters for a BORROWED
// element; and the borrowed case below (`pair + [s]`, `s` still live) drops its
// holder at the end of `main`, after every read, so a double free has nothing
// left to corrupt. Adding churn after the pushes does not change that. A
// fixture that observes the borrowed-element double free needs the holder to
// die BEFORE later allocations reuse the block, which is a different program
// shape than this one. An earlier draft of this comment claimed the reads below
// would catch a missing +1; they do not.
//
// WHY 20,000 AND NOT A MILLION. Size is not this file's evidence, and a big
// number here is not free: spec/wasm_cross fixtures are also run by the
// INTERPRETER (the third oracle, tests/wasm_runtime_test_parts/interp_leg.rs),
// whose `acc + [x]` is a real copy — measured quadratic, ~1 s at 20,000 and
// ~40 min per million-element build. A million here does not make the gate
// stronger, it makes every corpus binary hang. Wall-clock cost lives in
// scripts/check-perf-ratio.sh's strbuild rows instead.
fn build(n: Int, acc: List[String]) -> List[String] = {
  if n <= 0 then acc
  else build(n - 1, acc + [int.to_string(n)])
}

fn grow(n: Int) -> List[String] = {
  var out: List[String] = []
  var i = 0
  while i < n {
    out = out + [int.to_string(i)]
    i = i + 1
  }
  out
}

// Read EVERY element back. A credit lost on the way in shows up here as freed
// or reused bytes rather than as a number nobody looks at.
fn total_len(xs: List[String]) -> Int = {
  var sum = 0
  var i = 0
  let n = list.len(xs)
  while i < n {
    sum = sum + string.len(xs[i])
    i = i + 1
  }
  sum
}

fn main() -> Unit = {
  let tail = build(20000, [])
  let loop_form = grow(20000)
  println("${list.len(tail)} ${list.len(loop_form)}")
  println("${total_len(tail)} ${total_len(loop_form)}")
  println("${tail[0]} ${tail[19999]} ${loop_form[0]} ${loop_form[19999]}")
  // A borrowed element pushed from a var, twice: `s` owns a COMPUTED string (a
  // literal would live in the static pool, where a lost credit costs nothing)
  // and is still live after both pushes. The churn AFTER the pushes is what
  // makes a too-early free observable: a reused block would come back through
  // these reads as different bytes.
  let s = "c${list.len(tail)}"
  var pair: List[String] = []
  pair = pair + [s]
  pair = pair + [s]
  let churn = build(500, [])
  println("${s} ${list.join(pair, ",")} ${list.len(churn)} ${total_len(churn)}")
}
